 {:seon.task/name "an-entity-is-drawn-by-its-schemas-render"
  :seon.task/plan "Stage 2a of docs/users.md#the-stages-agreed-2026-11-09 (owner, 2026-11-09: \"No layout-of. No seon.render/layout I want to remove all the manual layout work. A hiccup render can override anywhere we are walking and rendering recursively. It's just more data that we are detecting and rendering\"; \"Entities should have renders IFF we want them rendered\"). Needs stage 1a (a context keyed by attributes, on main) and seon.repl/an-agent-draws-its-own-page merged.

What's wrong: a page is drawn by hand-written layouts that decide in code which keys get parts and in what order (seon.render.ui/render-ctx, user-ctx, root-ctx, task-list, conversation-list, page-chrome's lists), named by :seon.render/layout and read by seon.render/layout-of; four entities have two renders (task: task-view and task/status/citations; model call: model-call-view and conversation-turn; eval: evaluation and eval-item/eval-glance; message: message and message-hiccup); a cite, a doc section, a search, a result and a var with its verdict have no render and are drawn inside layout code; a cite's pull reached back to its agent, drawn again (Web's lab, 2026-11-09).

The change: the walk's root is the pulled entity drawn by its render (its own :seon.render/hiccup, else its entity schema's); a render returns its data and the walk draws each entity it holds by that entity's render, one component each, keyed by its path (seon.render/emit: a tracking view of an entity placed in hiccup draws as its render's component, :at its path); an explicit [#'f {} x] stays for a true layout; a bare {:db/id} draws as a link to where the entity is, with a model render naming its identity value. One render per entity schema; the missing ones written; eval tiles: an eval whose result's value has a render (its own key, or :seon.eval/schema's) is drawn in the sidebar by the result's render. The page-or-conversation split is Context's model renders (stage 2b). Deletes: seon.render/layout-of, :seon.render/layout, the layouts and their lists, each second render. Proofs: a page is the user's (or an agent's) pull drawn with no layout var; a change to one entity reruns its component alone (seon.render-cost-test/a-change-costs-its-components, its bound measured before and moved only on the owner's yes); no entity is drawn twice; test/seon/recorded_requests.edn unchanged where the data is, else named part by part with Context.

Experiment A, 2026-11-09 (Web, a demo root on a clone of runs/store, lab renders over a ratom shaped like ctx: an agent, its task, three evals): a render that returns its data, with each view of an entity it holds turned into a call of that entity's schema's render, the view the call's argument (a six-line helper standing in for one branch of seon.render/emit; seon.ratom/view= makes the key stable), draws each entity as its own component keyed by its render and path, with no layout. Changing one eval's code reran that eval's component alone; the task's plan, the task's alone; an eval added, the new eval and the agent's list. Estimate held: O(the entity that changed), plus a lookup a member for the list it is in. So stage 2a's mechanism is that one branch of emit; the rest of the stage is deleting layouts and writing the missing renders.

The one-walk spike, 2026-11-09 (Web, branch jolt/one-walk at b823b4c71b, not for landing; src +140 -30). Owner: \"render both the hiccup and the agents context as the processing of the pull\"; \"The pull doesn't need to be in order the render functions for the data being processed can order them\"; \"look up what something is the same way our tx processor looks up and validates data\"; \"No magic functions\". Built and proved (tests one-walk-renders-a-pull-to-hiccup-html-and-messages and one-walk-a-turns-diff-is-rendered-not-stored, green):
- Recognition by seon.db/entity-schemas (the write check's index), and one lookup naming an entity's render for every output (entity-render in the spike).
- In seon.render/render!, an entity's model render runs after its hiccup in the same run, reads tracked together; its output holds messages and the child entities it places, each the child's component (the one the hiccup placed, a cache hit), kept as a marker; the request is the root's output with each marker replaced by its child's, as seon.render/resolved does for hiccup (two lines).
- The agent's hiccup and model renders both order the turns, from one helper.
- One eval changed reruns its component alone, page and model; a new turn on a live walk reruns 3 components (the agent, the call, the eval) and leaves the earlier messages the same bytes.
Measured (seon.cost/measure, test world): a new turn costs O(turns): 0.65M ticks at 10 turns, 2.29M at 40, 9.17M at 160 (one turn added and taken back); the renders, the message read and the ctx diff are a seventh of it, the rest the walk's cache-hit path checking each earlier child, twice (hiccup and model output). Rendered from nothing (no cache): 1.5M at 10, 5.7M at 40, 22.8M at 160 (about 70 ms). So the cache is never relied on; a cheaper hit path (no contracted calls, the second placement reusing the first's key) is a later cost task, measured on a real session first.
To do here, beyond the spike: recognition by the best match (attributes held per schema, inherited counted, a tie an error value) instead of seon.db/most-specific's extends-all rule; the (and (map? v) (:db/id v)) check goes (a map under a ref attribute is an entity: the attribute it came through says what it reaches, seon.db/targets); seon.db/parents, extends?, entity-schemas and their readers take the registry they read (the branch's, as seon.db/index-of gives the write check), where today they read the binary's while seon.contract/properties reads the branch's; renders named by data (a value's own :seon.render/hiccup, a schema's through seon.render/render-of) run as components, so seon.web.html/expand runs none and an eval's glance stops staying stale after its render is redefined; the spike's agent render becomes the agent's :seon.render/hiccup (seon.render.ui/agent's layout list goes).
It reds 11 tests: 10 because root's entity holds root's attributes and the seon.config.* keys (by the best match, the config): stage 3's config entity fixes the data; one, seon.render-test/one-path-to-the-model, holds the :wire parts' old law, which 2b deletes.

Where to start (Web, 2026-11-09 evening, for a fresh session): the spike branch jolt/one-walk (b823b4c71b) holds the mechanism and two tests; it is a lab, so take its pieces onto a branch from main after Optimization's piece A (the index derived from the db) lands, and build in this order, each proved: (1) the walk renders the outputs asked of it, data on the walk ({:outputs #{:seon.render/hiccup :seon.render/wire}}): render! runs an entity's :seon.render/wire render only when the walk asks for it, since a render of page parts at a past point asked for both and recursed (Context measured 2.0 billion ticks at 10 turns, exponential); (2) an entity's renders in one component run, the model output's placed entities and [#'f {} & args] calls each run as a component keyed by render and args (the spike's wire-emit places entity views only: extend it to calls; Context's page-diff-wire is placed this way, so the walk's own component cache holds each turn's pair); (3) the request is the root's model output with each placed component's output in its place (the spike's seon.render/wire-of, two lines); (4) recognition by the best match (attributes held per entity schema, inherited counted, a tie an error value), with the index the walk holds, and no (:db/id v) check; (5) a render takes what it reads: my.render/render-ctx (pull db), a diff (db agent p0 p1); seon.turn/show-page!, the stored page evals and the old zero-arity callers go with 2b's landing; (6) the agent's page parts render (the spike's agent-parts, with :seon.ctx/pattern as metadata on its var: what it reads, so a point's parts are pulled by that and not the agent's whole pattern); (7) layouts deleted, second renders merged, the missing renders written, eval tiles; and the 15 renders that call hiccup or model themselves (Optimization's :seon.render/index list) changed to place values instead, so :seon.render/index goes. Measured in the spike (test world): a cold render of 160 turns, page and messages, 22.8M ticks (about 70 ms); a new turn on a live walk reruns 3 components; Context's model renders through it are linear in turns (64M ticks a request at 160 turns, 0.36M a new pair). Also Web's: the renders for unmerged entries and hold reasons (stage 5) and the user agent's walk (stage 4).

Built, 2026-11-09 evening (Web, branch jolt/walk-2a in .worktrees/walk-2a, from main with jolt/index-arg merged, its first commit after 0410a323e6): steps (1)-(4) of the order above. A walk renders the outputs asked of it, data on the walk map (:outputs, a set; absent or #{:seon.render/hiccup} runs no model render); seon.render/render! runs an entity's :seon.render/wire render after its hiccup in the same run, and its output places entities (by seon.render/entity-render) and [f opts & args] calls as components, a placed call running with :seon.render/as :seon.render/wire so its answer (messages) is its model output; seon.render/wire-of reads the root's model output with each marker replaced by its component's; seon.db/most-specific is the best match (attributes held per schema, inherited counted, a tie {:ambiguous [...]}); seon.render/schema-of uses it over every attribute; seon.render/props reads a schema's properties from the index's :forms, so render-for, declared and model-declared read the same index recognition does (no dynamic registry). Proof: seon.render-test/one-pass-renders-the-page-and-the-models-messages, on a lab schema in a branch's index (lab-agent, lab-call, lab-eval and their wire renders, a placed lab-turn-open call): recognition, a tie, each component, html, messages in order, one eval changed reruns its component alone, a new turn runs 4 components and leaves the earlier 6 messages the same bytes, a page-only walk runs no model render. Full prove on it (2026-11-09 evening): 12 tests and 28 check steps red, from two causes. (a) Expected: root's entity reads as the config by the best match, so root's render proc throws \"no entity schema of this record names a layout\" (seon.render/layout-of) and every check step that boots a root fails, with seon.root-test and seon.agent-test's root tests; it waits on Optimization's config entity (seon.config/the-config-in-force-is-its-own-entity), or 2a deletes layout-of first. (b) Ours to fix before landing: four seon.render-cost-test proofs are 1-6% over their call bounds (a-change-costs-its-components 3,720 calls against 3,660; the-printer-renders-nested-values-in-one-pass 422 against 400; a-restart-draws-its-turns-in-one-walk 3,421 against 3,400; a-member-gone-costs-the-lists-lookups), because seon.render/schema-of now calls seon.db/most-specific for every map (before, only for an entity with two identity attributes) and emit calls seon.render/entity-render for every view: fold the lookups into one uncontracted step in emit, or a single lookup by the index's :entity-schemas a key, and measure again; a bound moves only with the owner's yes. Also red: seon.main-test/no-docstring-carries-a-date (a docstring this branch wrote: find and fix). (b) fixed on jolt/walk-2a (839e8318df): the model output's work (the lookup of an entity's model render, the placing closure, the children held once) runs only in a component run of a walk that asks for :seon.render/wire, never on a cache hit; schema-of keeps a single owning schema a lookup with no call and recognizes a rendered schema by the index's :drawn-schemas (now every entity schema that declares a render, not only identity ones); a view is never an element's attributes by a type check with no contracted call. seon.render-cost-test, seon.db-test and the 2a proof are green, no bound moved; the docstrings cite undated sections. What still reds its prove: root's entity reads as the config (cause (a)), waiting on the config entity. Later on jolt/walk-2a (tip 5d205af272): seon.render/render-pull (pull db f ix outputs [opts]), a pull rendered in one pass and let go, how a past point is read; its root may be a model output's call (opts {:seon.render/as :seon.render/wire}), whose answer is the model output, each entity it places rendered by its own :seon.render/wire, and a call's answer wins over the root entity's own model render. Context's 2b is on jolt/model-wire-2a (caf7f678c1), built from walk-2a's tip with no spike: its byte-for-byte proof of the request is green through this walk on both worlds (175 tests in wire, render, render-cost and ctx); example sessions go through render-pull with a root call; a model render's opts carry :seon.render/index. Next: land the two branches together after the config entity (Optimization's erase window), then step (5)'s deletions with 2b's (show-page!, the stored page evals, :wire readers), then (6)-(7). State 2026-11-10 (Web, jolt/walk-2a): main merged in; Context's 10f0e428b2 taken (a model render's opts carry :seon.render/index); the model render's schema property is :seon.render/openai-compat and the model output's reader seon.render/openai-compat (was wire-of), agreed with Context so 2b renames none of the interface; the 2a proof red with the model render disabled (4 assertions), green with it. Step (6) built: seon.render.ui/agent-parts (was task-list), a render of the agent's pull with its :seon.ctx/pattern as var metadata, what a turn's diff compares (Context's stand-in goes at 2b's merge). The owner's order (2026-11-10): the config entity (jolt/config-entity, on the erase window) lands first, then 2a whole with the best match; a split without it is kept unlanded on jolt/walk-2a-split. Left: (7a) renders for a cite, a doc section, a search, a result and a var with its verdict, the cite's after the erase window's :seon.cite/target, designed with Context since the model reads them; (7b) the four second renders merged, with Context where the model reads them; (7c) the layouts and layout-of deleted, with 2b's openai-compat renders, since the conversation reaches the model through conversation-list's :wire marks until then."
